High-Level Assessment
I agree with the sync-time classification + runtime filtering approach. It is a natural extension of your existing architecture and aligns with how your system is supposed to work:

You already have a tool router validation step that should gate what the LLM can see and execute.

You already have dynamic tool assignment and task-to-tool mapping in the design docs, which implies intent-based selection rather than token overlap.

Claude’s design turns those architectural goals into a concrete action-level capability system, which is the right direction.

What I’d Keep As-Is
✅ Sync-time classification
Classifying once and caching metadata locally matches the centralized registry concept and avoids runtime API calls. It’s consistent with the platform’s “single source of truth” tool registry idea.

✅ Capability taxonomy
A stable taxonomy helps convert vague user intent into deterministic tool eligibility. This is consistent with the “task-to-tool mapping” pattern already documented, just at finer granularity (action vs. tool).

✅ Runtime filtering in the tool router
Your architecture already calls out a router validation step before the registry resolves and executes tools. This is the right place to enforce “only actions tagged message.send can be used for send intent.”

What I’d Adjust Before Implementing
1) Make the tool router the enforcement gate (not just selection)
Claude’s design focuses on filtering actions for the LLM. I’d go one step further: enforce that execution is also blocked unless action capabilities match intent. This ensures correctness even if the model “hallucinates” an action.

This aligns with the repo’s intended tool router validation step.

2) Keep “capabilities” distinct from “destructive”
I’d avoid overloading “capabilities” with risk classification. Use:

capabilities: semantic affordances (message.send, channel.list)

risk_level or destructive flags: safety policy

That separation makes the router logic clearer and more deterministic.

3) Don’t require LLM classification for every capability
Start with simple heuristic mapping (name/description keyword + allowlist per app) and only use LLM classification for ambiguous cases. This reduces cost and makes the system more maintainable early on, while still enabling manual overrides.

This also preserves the philosophy of a centralized tool registry with predictable metadata, as described in the docs.

4) Add a fallback tier for “no capability match”
When the intent classifier fails or the capability filter yields zero actions, you should:

either ask a clarification question, or

fall back to a safe “search/list” capability if applicable.

This avoids dead-ends and keeps the interaction robust.

5) Unify tool-loop behavior
Your architecture implies a single path: API → router validation → registry → executor. If multiple loops exist, they must all read from the same metadata table and enforce the same rules. Otherwise you’ll still see randomness and mismatched tool-start payloads.

Final Recommendation Before Implementation
Implement Claude’s structure with two additional constraints:

Validation must be enforced at execution time, not just selection time.

Capabilities should be deterministic and minimally LLM-dependent. Use LLM classification only where needed, and keep a manual override path.

This keeps the system aligned with the documented tool router/registry flow and makes it robust at scale.

Summary
Claude’s structure is solid and aligns with your system architecture.

I’d harden it by enforcing capability checks at execution, splitting semantic “capabilities” from safety/risk metadata, and reducing LLM reliance during classification.

This is consistent with the repo’s centralized tool registry and tool router validation design.

